Chapter: PrimArith
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Tier 7: integers, bits, and the number formatter. Chosen by counting, not by
 taste. A frequency pass over `whole-subject.codex` -- 2.6 MB of real compiler
 across 55,682 lines -- against what the tiers already assert found 606 builtin
 call sites with no coverage anywhere, and this file takes the arithmetic half
 of them: `bit-and` at 177 uses, `int-mod` at 71, `bit-or` at 55, `max` at 39,
 none of them tested by anything until now.

 INTEGER-TO-TEXT IS THE INSTRUMENT, AND IT HAD NEVER BEEN THE SUBJECT. Every
 cost row in every other tier prints through it -- 110 uses across the tier
 files -- and one of this project's own rules is to assert the instrument
 before believing it. Nobody had. If it disagrees between arms then every
 number in `primitive-costs.md` is suspect, so it is asserted here first and
 by ROUND TRIP: `text-to-integer (integer-to-text n) == n` needs neither arm's
 formatter to be trusted, only the two to be inverse. Digit counts are checked
 beside it, because a round trip alone would pass if both directions were
 wrong in the same way.

 The plug's formatter is hand-rolled and worth suspecting: it prints through
 zig's own `{d}` into ASCII and then maps each byte to CCE by hand --
 `if (cx_ch == '-') 73 else 3 + (cx_ch - '0')`. Two constants, one branch, and
 a whole tier's numbers riding on it.

 Sign is where integer code goes wrong, so every operator below is asked about
 negatives explicitly. `int-mod` is the one with a documented answer: bare
 metal takes `idiv`'s remainder and then ADDS the divisor's magnitude if the
 result came out negative (`emit-int-mod-reg-locals`,
 Emit/X86_64Builtins.codex:440-455), so the answer is Euclidean and never
 negative. A truncating implementation agrees on positives and disagrees on
 exactly half the inputs, which is why the negative rows matter more than the
 positive ones.

 The bit operators are asked about negatives for the same reason: they are
 i64, so `bit-and n (0 - 1)` is a sign-extension question, not a masking one.

 EDGES LIVE ELSEWHERE. `abs` at the minimum integer, `__narrow`, and the
 out-of-range shifts have no negation, no representation and no answer
 respectively, and each kills one arm. A tier file cannot hold a trap -- one
 takes the other forty assertions with it -- so those are
 `probe-arith-edges.codex` and `probe-shift-count.codex`.

Section: Instrument

  show-yn : Boolean -> Text
  show-yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

Section: Shapes

 A round trip that trusts neither direction, only that they invert.

  round-trips : Integer -> Boolean
  round-trips (n) = text-to-integer (integer-to-text n) == n

  digits : Integer -> Integer
  digits (n) = text-length (integer-to-text n)

Section: Main

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni "-- the instrument: integer-to-text, asserted before it is believed --"
   print-line-uni ("round trip 0               : " & show-yn (round-trips 0))
   print-line-uni ("round trip 1               : " & show-yn (round-trips 1))
   print-line-uni ("round trip -1              : " & show-yn (round-trips (0 - 1)))
   print-line-uni ("round trip 7               : " & show-yn (round-trips 7))
   print-line-uni ("round trip -7              : " & show-yn (round-trips (0 - 7)))
   print-line-uni ("round trip 1000000         : " & show-yn (round-trips 1000000))
   print-line-uni ("round trip -1000000        : " & show-yn (round-trips (0 - 1000000)))
   print-line-uni ("round trip 4611686018427387904: " & show-yn (round-trips 4611686018427387904))
   print-line-uni ("digits of 0 is 1           : " & show-yn (digits 0 == 1))
   print-line-uni ("digits of 7 is 1           : " & show-yn (digits 7 == 1))
   print-line-uni ("digits of -7 is 2          : " & show-yn (digits (0 - 7) == 2))
   print-line-uni ("digits of 1000000 is 7     : " & show-yn (digits 1000000 == 7))
   print-line-uni ("digits of -1000000 is 8    : " & show-yn (digits (0 - 1000000) == 8))
   print-line-uni ("minus sign is one character: " & show-yn (digits (0 - 7) == digits 7 + 1))
   print-line-uni ("zero has no sign           : " & show-yn (text-compare (integer-to-text 0) (integer-to-text (0 - 0)) == 0))
   print-line-uni "-- int-mod: Euclidean on both arms, or half the inputs disagree --"
   print-line-uni ("7 mod 3                    : " & integer-to-text (int-mod 7 3))
   print-line-uni ("-7 mod 3                   : " & integer-to-text (int-mod (0 - 7) 3))
   print-line-uni ("7 mod -3                   : " & integer-to-text (int-mod 7 (0 - 3)))
   print-line-uni ("-7 mod -3                  : " & integer-to-text (int-mod (0 - 7) (0 - 3)))
   print-line-uni ("0 mod 5                    : " & integer-to-text (int-mod 0 5))
   print-line-uni ("-1 mod 8                   : " & integer-to-text (int-mod (0 - 1) 8))
   print-line-uni ("never negative             : " & show-yn (int-mod (0 - 7) 3 >= 0))
   print-line-uni ("agrees on positives        : " & show-yn (int-mod 17 5 == 2))
   print-line-uni "-- max and min --"
   print-line-uni ("max 3 9                    : " & integer-to-text (max 3 9))
   print-line-uni ("max -3 -9                  : " & integer-to-text (max (0 - 3) (0 - 9)))
   print-line-uni ("min 3 9                    : " & integer-to-text (min 3 9))
   print-line-uni ("min -3 -9                  : " & integer-to-text (min (0 - 3) (0 - 9)))
   print-line-uni ("max of equals              : " & show-yn (max 4 4 == 4))
   print-line-uni ("min across zero            : " & show-yn (min (0 - 1) 1 == (0 - 1)))
   print-line-uni "-- abs, away from the edge (the edge is in probe-arith-edges) --"
   print-line-uni ("abs 5                      : " & integer-to-text (abs 5))
   print-line-uni ("abs -5                     : " & integer-to-text (abs (0 - 5)))
   print-line-uni ("abs 0                      : " & integer-to-text (abs 0))
   print-line-uni "-- bits: i64, so negatives are a sign-extension question --"
   print-line-uni ("and 12 10                  : " & integer-to-text (bit-and 12 10))
   print-line-uni ("or 12 10                   : " & integer-to-text (bit-or 12 10))
   print-line-uni ("xor 12 10                  : " & integer-to-text (bit-xor 12 10))
   print-line-uni ("and n -1 is n              : " & show-yn (bit-and 12345 (0 - 1) == 12345))
   print-line-uni ("or n 0 is n                : " & show-yn (bit-or 12345 0 == 12345))
   print-line-uni ("xor n n is 0               : " & show-yn (bit-xor 12345 12345 == 0))
   print-line-uni ("xor n 0 is n               : " & show-yn (bit-xor 12345 0 == 12345))
   print-line-uni ("and of two negatives       : " & integer-to-text (bit-and (0 - 12) (0 - 10)))
   print-line-uni ("or of two negatives        : " & integer-to-text (bit-or (0 - 12) (0 - 10)))
   print-line-uni ("xor of two negatives       : " & integer-to-text (bit-xor (0 - 12) (0 - 10)))
   print-line-uni ("and -1 -1                  : " & integer-to-text (bit-and (0 - 1) (0 - 1)))
   print-line-uni ("xor -1 -1                  : " & integer-to-text (bit-xor (0 - 1) (0 - 1)))
   print-line-uni ("or -1 0                    : " & integer-to-text (bit-or (0 - 1) 0))
   print-line-uni ("low byte of a negative     : " & integer-to-text (bit-and (0 - 1) 255))
   print-line-uni ("masking clears the sign    : " & show-yn (bit-and (0 - 1) 255 == 255))
   print-line-uni "-- the identities that must hold whatever the representation --"
   print-line-uni ("de Morgan via xor -1       : " & show-yn (bit-xor (bit-and 12 10) (0 - 1) == bit-or (bit-xor 12 (0 - 1)) (bit-xor 10 (0 - 1))))
   print-line-uni ("and is commutative         : " & show-yn (bit-and 12 10 == bit-and 10 12))
   print-line-uni ("or distributes over and    : " & show-yn (bit-or 12 (bit-and 10 6) == bit-and (bit-or 12 10) (bit-or 12 6)))
   print-line-uni ("shift then mask round trips: " & show-yn (bit-and (bit-shr (bit-shl 200 8) 8) 255 == 200))
  end
